Spacecraft Constellation Fault Simulator

A GUI-based simulator built on the Basilisk astrodynamics framework. It models multi-satellite constellations with cluster management, realistic fault injection, ML-based fault detection, DRL-based task reassignment, and 3D playback via Vizard.

Project path in repository: ProjectWork/BskSim/ (repo: CompProjectA/satelliteConstellation-basilisk). 
GitHub

Version: 1.0
Updated: 31 Oct 2025
Basilisk: 1.18.x
Python: 3.10+

Overview

This simulator is designed for research and teaching on autonomous constellation operations and fault tolerance. It lets you:

Configure clusters and formations (line, triangle, diamond, leader–follower).

Inject realistic faults (friction, power limit, encoder, battery).

Detect anomalies using an autoencoder model; explain detections with SHAP/LIME.

Reassign tasks intelligently with PPO (primary), plus DQN and TDHD options.

Export 3D playback binaries for Vizard.

Key Features

Constellation

LEO/MEO/GEO support, up to 4 clusters, 2–6 satellites each.

Leader–follower hierarchy and quick-setup presets.

Formation geometry and inter-satellite spacing controls.

Faults

Friction, Power-Limit, Encoder, Battery.

One-shot and periodic injection; per-satellite or cluster-wide.

Detection & Recovery

ML autoencoder anomaly detection with confidence scores.

XAI explanations (optional).

PPO-driven task reassignment; safe-mode fallback.

Analysis & Viz

Attitude/power/RW telemetry plots; formation and link metrics.

Vizard .bin export for fast 3D review.

System Requirements

OS: Windows 10/11 (macOS/Linux possible with Basilisk installed).

Python: 3.10+

RAM: 8 GB minimum (16 GB recommended)

Disk: ≥ 5 GB free (logs/models/plots)

